<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/manhattan.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/manhattan-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/manhattan.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/where-we-appraise/">Locations</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Manhattan</span></nav><p class="kicker">New York City</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Manhattan, New York</h1><p class="lede">When you need an insurance appraiser in Manhattan, the dispute is rarely about a whole building; it is usually about one apartment, one leak and two or three overlapping policies. We value the amount of loss for co-op shareholders, condo owners and building owners when the carrier’s number and the real cost of repair do not match.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>New York County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>New York City</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="local-card"><div><p class="lc-t">Local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Water leaks from units above</li><li>Fire &amp; smoke migration</li><li>Coastal surge (flood)</li><li>Burst risers &amp; pipes</li><li>Facade &amp; roof wind damage</li></ul></div><div><p class="lc-t">Housing mix</p><p>Manhattan is dominated by co-op and condominium apartments in pre-war and post-war buildings, with a smaller stock of brownstones, walk-ups and rental towers.</p></div><div><p class="lc-t">Areas we visit</p><p>Upper West Side, Upper East Side, Harlem, Washington Heights, Greenwich Village, Tribeca, Battery Park City, Lower East Side</p></div></div>

<h2>Vertical living, layered policies</h2>
<p>An insurance appraiser in Manhattan spends far more time inside apartments than on rooftops. A failed toilet supply line on the 14th floor can soak three units below, and each of those owners may be dealing with a different carrier, a co-op or condo master policy, and a board that controls which contractors may enter the building. Before anyone argues about price, the scope has to be split: what belongs to the building and what belongs to the unit owner. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/co-op-condo-claim-splitter/">co-op and condo claim splitter</a> is a useful starting point for that conversation.</p>
<h2>Storms the island remembers</h2>
<p>Superstorm Sandy on October 29, 2012 pushed surge into Lower Manhattan, flooding basements, mechanical rooms and electrical vaults from Battery Park City to the East Village and knocking out power below roughly 39th Street for days. In September 2021 the remnants of Ida dropped record hourly rainfall in Central Park, overwhelming drains and sending water into lower-level units and storage rooms. Flood damage is generally excluded from homeowners and many building policies, so separating surge or overland water from covered wind, rain intrusion or sewer backup (if endorsed) matters a great deal.</p>
<h2>What the estimates tend to miss</h2>
<p>Pre-war interiors carry plaster on wire lath or terra-cotta block, solid-wood millwork, herringbone floors and custom cabinetry. Carrier estimates written from generic line items often price drywall and stock trim instead. Building logistics add cost too: freight-elevator scheduling, certificate-of-insurance requirements, daytime-only work rules, debris carry-down and protection of common corridors.</p>
<ul>
<li>Plaster and specialty finishes priced as standard drywall.</li>
<li>Alteration-agreement and building-rule costs left out entirely.</li>
<li>Matching of continuous floors or finishes through undamaged rooms.</li>
<li>Smoke and soot cleaning limited to the room of origin when odor travelled through shafts.</li>
<li>Disagreement over whether a riser leak is a building or unit-owner item.</li>
</ul>
<p>Timing is its own problem in Manhattan. Board approvals, contractor insurance requirements and building work hours can stretch a simple repair over months, and temporary housing or additional living expense can grow while the scope is argued. A clear, itemized estimate that reflects how work actually gets done in your building is usually the fastest way to narrow the gap.</p>
<h2>Landmarks, facades and the LPC</h2>
<p>Large parts of Manhattan sit in historic districts overseen by the Landmarks Preservation Commission, from the Upper West Side to SoHo. Exterior work on a designated building may need LPC approval and period-appropriate materials, which can raise repair cost; whether a policy pays that extra depends on its ordinance-or-law terms, so check your policy. We work across Harlem, Washington Heights, Inwood, both East and West Sides, Midtown, Chelsea, the Village, Tribeca and the Financial District. For the master-policy versus HO-6 question, see our guide to <a href="/policyholder-guides/co-op-condo-master-policy-vs-ho6/">co-op and condo coverage</a>, and our <a href="/claim-appraisals/co-op-condo-commercial-appraisal/">co-op, condo and commercial appraisal</a> service.</p>

<h2>Appraisal services for Manhattan properties</h2>
<p>Every New York Insurance Appraiser service is available in Manhattan and across New York County, including:</p>
<ul class="cols"><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/appraisal-panel-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/homeowners-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowners Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/co-op-condo-commercial-appraisal/">Co-op, Condo &amp; Commercial Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/roof-hail-damage-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/windstorm-tornado-appraisal/">Windstorm &amp; Tornado Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/hurricane-noreaster-appraisal/">Hurricane &amp; Nor’easter Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/burst-pipe-water-damage-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/claim-appraisals/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Manhattan claim questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I invoke appraisal if my co-op board is also making a claim?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Often yes. Your own policy’s appraisal clause applies to the amount of loss under your policy. The building’s claim under its master policy is separate. Coordinating scopes early helps avoid paying for the same item twice or leaving a gap.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide whether my insurer has to cover a leak?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Appraisal generally sets the dollar amount of a covered loss. Questions about whether something is covered at all are coverage issues; for those, talk to a New York attorney.</p></div></details></div>
